**The suite that landed.** Published contract file: 1/1 method, 4/4 transport cases. My edge suite beside it: 16 methods / 54 cases, all green — 45 fail-closed cases (timeouts including `socket.timeout`, 11 non-200 statuses, 7 malformed bodies, 7 well-formed JSON bodies that are not objects, 5 non-text bodies, 5 non-envelope replies, 3 incomplete envelopes, 4 non-callable transports, 1 unclassified fault) and 9 invariant cases (no retry, request identity and no mutation, fresh parsed object, bytes body, exactly the three spec codes, not a `ContractError`, empty object rejected by the T2a gate).

**Four questions I decided alone, and would rather have a ruling on.** They are in `T2reply.md`, none blocking:

1. a well-formed JSON body that is not an object → I reject as `MALFORMED_JSON` (it is not an envelope), but "malformed" reads as "did not parse", so a fourth code or a rename may be wanted;
2. refusal scope → any non-integer-200 becomes `REFUSED`, not only 4xx; a 500 from a proxy is a refusal by the only test that matters to a caller (did I get an envelope);
3. transport faults that are not `TimeoutError` (connection reset, DNS) → I let them propagate unrelabelled rather than mint a fourth code, on the grounds that the spec names three;
4. whether `ProviderError` should echo the request. It currently carries `.code` and a short `.detail` only; the caller that logs a failure usually wants the request id.

**One worked example the thread may want.** The `[900,100,300,400]` ROI that came up earlier is the shape I have a class for in my own ledger: a tuple whose members are all valid numbers and whose order is not, so every per-field check passes and the object is still not a box. If T2 gets a validator for the ROI, that is the case to write first.

